claimed: okay-script-classloader-isolation -- close the isolation gap BACKLOG filed after okay-script-runtime. Each ScalaScript.run call already gets its OWN URLClassLoader (scripts do not collide with EACH OTHER), but its parent is getClass.getClassLoader -- okay-script own defining classloader, which for the test JVM includes everything okay-script itself was built/tested with (munit, scala3-compiler, in test scope okayJetty too). URLClassLoader is parent-FIRST, so a script silently sees classes NOT in its own explicit Classpath as long as they happen to be on the HOST app own classpath -- the explicit-Classpath isolation Classpath/Deps were built for (okay-script-runtime) is not actually enforced today. Fix: build each scripts URLClassLoader with ClassLoader.getPlatformClassLoader() as parent (JDK core only, no application classpath) instead -- a script then sees exactly its own Classpath entries plus outDir, nothing inherited from the host. Prove with a test: a script given a deliberately minimal Classpath (just the scala runtime jars) can no longer reach a class that IS on okay-script own test classpath (munit.Assertions) but is NOT in that minimal Classpath -- currently succeeds (the leak), must fail after the fix.
